  "body": {
    "id": "thm:constructive-rounding-upper",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "statement": "For every \\(N\\ge4\\) and every bounded empirical marginal triple \\(F\\), there exists \\(T\\in\\mathcal S_N(F)\\) with average cost at most \\(V_{\\mathrm{rel}}(F)+402/N\\); therefore \\(V_{\\mathrm{int}}(F)-V_{\\mathrm{rel}}(F)\\le\\min\\{1/4,402/N\\}\\).  The analytic term \\(402/N\\) is strictly smaller than the trivial bound \\(1/4\\) exactly when \\(N\\ge1609\\).  If the atoms and an optimal coupling \\(\\pi^\\star\\) are rational, \\(\\mathsf A_{\\mathrm{round}}\\) returns such a schedule, equivalently two permutations relative to arm 1, in time polynomial in \\(N\\) and the rational input bit length.  The vertex extraction, comparisons, and output use exact rational arithmetic.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "N",
      "F",
      "T_{\\mathrm{alg}}",
      "\\mathcal S_N(F)",
      "V_{\\mathrm{rel}}(F)",
      "V_{\\mathrm{int}}(F)",
      "\\pi^\\star",
      "\\mathsf A_{\\mathrm{round}}"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "def:realizable-value",
      "def:rounding-algorithm",
      "lem:quadratic-slack-accounting",
      "lem:hierarchical-repair",
      "def:fractional-value",
      "thm:fixed-contrast-support-dichotomy"
    ],
    "justification": "This is the exact \\(K=3\\), fixed-causal-contrast specialization of the arbitrary-support multiscale construction, retaining the trial-facing constant \\(402\\) and bit-polynomial implementation guarantee.",
    "gap": "Emami and Pass (2025, Discussion) ask for quantitative Monge approximation, and Haus (2015) supplies bottleneck approximations; neither proves a uniform additive same-empirical-marginal squared-cost rate with bit-polynomial construction.",
    "consumer": "A trial analyst can turn a fractional transport optimum into one realizable \\(N\\)-unit completion and report both the actual primal--dual width and the universal \\(402/N\\) fallback.",
    "proof_tex": "Theorem \\(\\mathrm{thm{:}fixed\\mbox{-}contrast\\mbox{-}support\\mbox{-}dichotomy}\\), specialized as in (25), and Lemma \\(\\mathrm{lem{:}quadratic\\mbox{-}slack\\mbox{-}accounting}\\) give the explicit schedule \\(T_{\\mathrm{alg}}\\in\\mathcal S_N(F)\\) with\n\\[\n C_{\\mathrm{alg}}(F)<V_{\\mathrm{rel}}(F)+\\frac{402}{N}. \\tag{34}\n\\]\nEvery realizable schedule induces a coupling in \\(\\Gamma(F)\\), whereas \\(T_{\\mathrm{alg}}\\) is feasible for \\(V_{\\mathrm{int}}(F)\\).  Hence\n\\[\n V_{\\mathrm{rel}}(F)\\le V_{\\mathrm{int}}(F)\\le C_{\\mathrm{alg}}(F)\n <V_{\\mathrm{rel}}(F)+\\frac{402}{N}. \\tag{35}\n\\]\nOn \\([0,1]^3\\), the fixed contrast lies in \\([-1/2,1/2]\\), so \\(0\\le C(y)\\le1/4\\).  Combining this range with (35) proves the stated minimum of the two bounds.  Since \\(402/N<1/4\\) exactly when \\(N>1608\\), the analytic term is nontrivial exactly for integer \\(N\\ge1609\\).\n\nThe specialized algorithm is precisely \\(\\mathsf A_{\\mathrm{round}}\\) in Definition \\(\\mathrm{def{:}rounding\\mbox{-}algorithm}\\).  Lemma \\(\\mathrm{lem{:}hierarchical\\mbox{-}repair}\\) proves exact marginals and polynomially many exact rational operations.  Expanding the output into \\(N\\) labeled arm-1 occurrences, the arm-2 and arm-3 occurrence labels are two permutations, even with repeated values.  This proves all algorithmic assertions.",
